<p>The site's two themes (this one, which every site shares, and the site's own) <strong>update themselves</strong>. Nobody needs to do anything. Theme Settings &gt; <a href="{{ go.licence }}"><strong>Licence &amp; Updates</strong></a> shows where things stand.</p>
{{ shot( 'updates', 'Licence & Updates: each theme\'s version, licence and updates.' ) }}

<h3>Status tab</h3>
<ul class="guide-list">
	<li><strong>Licence</strong>: the site's licence and until when it is valid. Updates need a valid licence.</li>
	<li><strong>Updates</strong>: <em>Up to date</em>, or the new version waiting. New versions reach sites a few at a time over a short period, so the tab may say when this site gets it.</li>
	<li><strong>Check licence and updates now</strong> asks right away instead of waiting for the next automatic check.</li>
</ul>
<div class="guide-tip">If an update ever breaks the site, it is undone by itself and the site goes back to the previous version. The tab says so, and that version is never installed again.</div>

<h3>WordPress and plugin updates</h3>
<p>When the site's plan includes updates, your developer sees which WordPress and plugin updates are waiting and installs them from their management service, so you don't have to. WordPress's own protections apply: the site shows a short maintenance message while it updates, and a plugin that breaks the site is put back to its previous version.</p>

<h3>Backups tab</h3>
<p>When the site's plan includes backups, they are made and kept off the site automatically, and listed here. To restore one, click <strong>Download to WPvivid</strong> and ask your developer to restore it.</p>

<h3>Licence tab</h3>
<p>The licence key and the update server. They are filled in for you and cannot be edited here.</p>
<p>The plugins built into the theme (custom fields, SEO, admin tools and, for a shop, WooCommerce) are updated with the theme too. They never update on their own, so the site always runs a tested combination.</p>
